%S A401120 1,2,4,3,12,12,4,24,54,32,5,40,144,216,80,6,60,300,768,810,192,7,84,
%T A401120 540,2000,3840,2916,448,8,112,882,4320,12500,18432,10206,1024,9,144,
%U A401120 1344,8232,32400,75000,86016,34992,2304,10,180,1944,14336,72030,233280,437500,393216,118098,5120
%N A401120 Array A(n,k), n >= 1, k >= 2, read by antidiagonals: number of edges in the k^n cubic grid.
%C A401120 The k^n cubic grid has n coordinate directions. Each direction contains k^(n-1) parallel grid lines. Each line has k lattice points and k-1 edges. Thus, A(n,k) = n*k^(n-1)*(k-1). Equivalently, A(n,k) counts the number of unordered pairs of adjacent lattice points in the cubic grid.
%H A401120 Eddie Lin Rui, <a href="/A401120/b401120.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10011; the first 141 antidiagonals of the array</a>
%e A401120 The array begins:
%e A401120         k=2    k=3     k=4      k=5       k=6       k=7        k=8        k=9
%e A401120   n=1     1      2       3        4         5         6          7          8
%e A401120   n=2     4     12      24       40        60        84        112        144
%e A401120   n=3    12     54     144      300       540       882       1344       1944
%e A401120   n=4    32    216     768     2000      4320      8232      14336      23328
%e A401120   n=5    80    810    3840    12500     32400     72030     143360     262440
%e A401120   n=6   192   2916   18432    75000    233280    605052    1376256    2834352
%e A401120   n=7   448  10206   86016   437500   1632960   4941258   12845056   29760696
%e A401120   n=8  1024  34992  393216  2500000  11197440  39530064  117440512  306110016
%e A401120 For a 3-dimensional, 4 X 4 X 4 cubic lattice, each coordinate direction has 4 X 4 lines. Each line has 3 edges. Thus, total number of edges = 3*4*4*3 = 144.
%e A401120 Using the array formula, a(13) = A(3,4) = 3*4^(3-1)*(4-1) = 144.
%t A401120 A401120[n_, k_] := n*k^(n - 1)*(k - 1);
%t A401120 Table[A401120[m, n - m + 2], {n, 10}, {m, n}] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Oct 22 2026 *)
%o A401120 (Python)
%o A401120 def A(n, k):
%o A401120     return n*(k-1)*k**(n-1)
%o A401120 a = []
%o A401120 for d in range(1, 10):
%o A401120     a.extend(A(n, d + 2 - n) for n in range(1, d + 1))
%o A401120 print(a)
